<p>
  <a href="/articles/introducing-layr/">Introducing LAYR</a> describes what LAYR is
  for: a language in which the shape of the code is the shape of the page, the
  numbers are the design's numbers, and layout follows fixed rules. This article
  is about the machinery that makes those promises hold. It covers the compiler,
  Design Scale, the layout rules, and the system of ordered changes LAYR calls
  EIED. All of it is open source, and the file names below are the real ones.
</p>

<h2>From a file to a page</h2>
<p>
  LAYR is compiler-first. A project's <code>.layr</code> files and its
  <code>layr.yaml</code> go through five stages, and what comes out is ordinary
  web output: one JavaScript module and one stylesheet per source file, plus a
  global stylesheet with the base layout rules and the Design Scale variables.
</p>

<Figure caption="The compiler's stages. Every stage reports problems as diagnostics with a code (L1011, L3102 and so on), many with an automatic fix, and the same diagnostics drive the CLI, the editor and the AI tooling." wide>
  <Pipeline />
</Figure>

<p>
  The parser is written by hand and returns a syntax tree together with a list of
  diagnostics, rather than giving up at the first mistake. That matters because
  the same compiler runs inside the editor, where code is half-typed most of the
  time. Analysis then works across the whole project: it resolves names, checks
  types, and builds a graph of every Export, Extract, Inject and Duplicate, so it
  knows which objects each part of the app reads and changes before anything
  runs.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Lowering</strong> is the step that turns a widget and its resolved
  configuration into CSS classes, declarations and element attributes. The same
  lowering code is used in two places. The compiler uses it to turn every static
  configuration into a CSS rule at build time, and the runtime uses it to turn
  configuration that changes while the page runs into inline styles. Because it
  is one implementation, a box written with a fixed value and a box whose value
  comes from state cannot disagree about what the value means.
</p>

<h3>One schema behind everything</h3>
<p>
  Every core widget, its configuration keys, its slots, its events, its accepted
  aliases and its documentation are described once, in a single schema. The
  compiler, the formatter, the analyzer, the language server, the API reference
  on the website and the Skill that teaches AI agents the language all read from
  it. When a widget gains a key, every one of those learns about it at the same
  time, and none of them can contradict another.
</p>

<h2>Design Scale, in plain CSS</h2>
<p>
  The most distinctive promise LAYR makes is that a number in a file is a pixel
  of the design. <code>w: 240</code> means 240 pixels of whichever design frame is
  active: by default a 390-pixel phone, an 834-pixel tablet, a 1440-pixel desktop
  or a 2560-pixel ultrawide, starting at 0, 600, 1024 and 1920 pixels of screen.
</p>
<p>
  It would be easy to implement this with JavaScript that measures the window
  and rescales everything on resize. LAYR does none of that. Design Scale compiles
  to CSS. The global stylesheet defines a custom property, <code>--ds</code>,
  registered as a length, with one value per frame inside a media query. Every
  design-pixel value becomes a <code>calc()</code> against it.
</p>

<CodeBlock code={css} lang="css" />

<p>
  A few details in that output are worth unpacking, because each one solves a
  real problem.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>The scale is clamped.</strong> Inside a frame, values follow the
    screen, but never below 0.9 or above 1.15 times their design size. Scaling
    fine-tunes; the frames do the real re-layout. The bounds are written in
    <code>rem</code>, so they move with the reader's font-size setting.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Phones and tablets measure their short side</strong>
    (<code>vmin</code>), so rotating a device does not rescale the whole page.
    Desktop frames use the window's width.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The variable holds a thousand design pixels, not one.</strong>
    Browsers store registered lengths with limited precision (Firefox rounds to a
    sixtieth of a pixel), and multiplying a rounded single pixel by 240 would
    multiply its rounding error too. Storing the length of 1000 design pixels and
    dividing at the point of use keeps the error out of sight.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Registering the property matters.</strong> Because <code>--ds</code>
    is registered as a length, it resolves once where it is declared and is
    inherited as an absolute length. A subtree can declare its own Design Scale,
    measured against its own width through container queries, without nested
    containers confusing which element the units refer to.
  </li>
</ul>

<Figure caption="The rendered width of w: 240 at every screen width, from LAYR's own formula with the default frames, phones and tablets held upright. Inside each frame it follows the screen within its clamp; at 390, 834, 1440 and 2560 it is exactly 240; where a new frame takes over, it steps back and starts again." wide>
  <ScaleChart />
</Figure>

<p>
  A value written for several frames, such as a heading that should be 32 on a
  phone and 64 on a desktop, is not switched at a breakpoint. Between the frames'
  design widths it follows a straight line in viewport width, with a
  <code>rem</code> term so that zooming still works. Numbers flow; structure
  (which way a row runs, whether something is shown) steps at frame boundaries.
  Font sizes scale more gently than lengths and keep part of their size in
  <code>rem</code>, and the analyzer warns (L6101) if a font's largest size is
  more than two and a half times its smallest, which keeps text zoomable to 200%
  as WCAG 1.4.4 requires.
</p>

<h2>The layout rules</h2>
<p>
  LAYR's layout model answers three questions for every object: how big is it,
  how are its children arranged, and what happens when there is not enough room.
</p>
<p>
  Size is one of three kinds. A <strong>fixed</strong> length is exactly that.
  <strong><code>hug</code></strong>, the default, fits the content.
  <strong><code>fill</code></strong> takes a share of the space the parent has
  left. Since LAYR 3.3, the last two can be computed with: <code>w: hug + 24</code>
  is the content's width plus 24, and it keeps tracking the content as the label
  changes. In browsers that support CSS <code>calc-size()</code>, currently
  Chrome and Edge, this is done entirely in CSS; in Firefox and Safari the runtime
  measures the starting size before paint.
</p>
<p>
  When a row runs out of room, it adapts in a fixed order rather than
  overflowing at random:
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Flexible content shrinks toward its minimum, and text wraps.</li>
  <li>
    Then it depends on what the row holds. A row of only hugging items, such as
    chips, buttons or links, wraps onto new lines. A row with a filling pane
    stacks into a column once that pane would be squeezed, and becomes a row
    again when there is room.
  </li>
  <li>
    Only if nothing can adapt, such as a fixed child wider than its fixed
    parent, does content clip, and the compiler warns about it (L2101).
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  The point is not that these rules are clever. It is that they are the same
  every time and written down, so a layout's behaviour can be predicted from
  its code, and <code>layr analyze</code> can explain any adaptation it made.
</p>

<h2>EIED: change anything, from anywhere, in order</h2>
<p>
  Real applications need one part of the page to affect another: a toolbar that
  changes a card's colour, a page that reads a widget's measured size. Most UI
  systems handle this with props passed down, context, or global state, each with
  its own rules. LAYR has one mechanism, four operations wide.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Export</strong> names what an object shares, like a small public interface.</li>
  <li><strong>Extract</strong> reads a feature, including measured ones such as rendered size.</li>
  <li><strong>Inject</strong> changes a feature.</li>
  <li><strong>Duplicate</strong> shows a copy of an object at a chosen point in its history.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Objects are <em>protected by default</em>: nothing outside an object's owner
  can change it. Using an Extract or Inject on a feature is the explicit act that
  makes it reachable, with nothing else to declare. Anything that must never be
  changed from outside is marked <code>!mut</code>, and an Inject against it is a
  compile error (L3101). The one escape hatch, a forced inject, is allowed only in
  files the project lists by name in <code>layr.yaml</code>, and the analyzer
  lists every use.
</p>
<p>
  Targets are addressed by id, or by a lookup path through the tree when an
  object has no name. The compiler resolves every address, so a path broken by
  an edit is an error (L3301) rather than a silent no-op.
</p>
<CodeBlock code={paths} lang="layr" />

<h3>Layers, not overwrites</h3>
<p>
  What makes EIED manageable is that changes are not overwrites. Each operation
  is a layer with a position, its <code>exeOrder</code>, in one chain per object.
  Layers are reversible, and two operations on the same object at the same order
  are an error (L3102) with the nearest free order offered as the fix. Every
  feature then resolves in a single documented order.
</p>

<Figure caption="The order every feature resolves in. Injections apply by exeOrder, and any feature's chain can be listed with layr chain, or traced to the line that wrote it with layr analyze --explain." wide>
  <Cascade />
</Figure>

<p>
  Because the chain is known to the compiler, it can be shown. <code>layr chain</code>
  lists the objects that operations change or read; naming one lists its layers in
  order with the value each writes and where it is written. In the editor,
  finding references on an id lists every Extract and Inject of that object. A
  question like "why is this padding 20 here?" has an answer that a tool can
  print.
</p>

<h2>Tools as first-class readers</h2>
<p>
  LAYR treats its tools, and AI agents, as readers of the code as important as
  people, and that shapes several decisions.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>One canonical form.</strong> The formatter accepts every alias and
    shorthand and writes one form, putting each object of a multi-line tree on its
    own line so the hierarchy reads down the indentation.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>An editor that keeps up.</strong> The language server answers
    completion from the last compile instead of recompiling on every keystroke.
    On the 23-file project that is LAYR's own website, that took completion from
    33 to 82 milliseconds per keystroke down to between 1 and 6.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The project's own version, everywhere.</strong> The global
    <code>layr</code> command, the VS Code extension and the agent tooling all run
    the LAYR a project has installed, so a project written for one version is
    never checked by the rules of another.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Agents get the language, not guesses.</strong> A command installs the
    LAYR Skill and an MCP server into a project, so an AI agent works from the same
    schema the compiler does.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Documentation that cannot rot.</strong> Every code snippet in the
    documentation is compiled in continuous integration.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>What LAYR borrows, and what it owns</h2>
<p>
  LAYR does not reinvent the parts of the web that already work. React renders
  the output, so React packages can be used inside LAYR and LAYR inside React
  apps. Vite runs the development server and the build. npm distributes it. The
  browser's own layout engine does the actual layout, because everything LAYR
  decides is expressed as CSS.
</p>
<p>
  What LAYR owns is meaning: what a number means, what happens when space runs
  out, who may change what, and in which order. Those are the questions that
  usually end up answered differently in every file of a project. Here they are
  answered once, by a compiler, in a way that tools can check and explain.
</p>
